WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.920
Elephants are so different
from us anatomically

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.720
that you would think there
would be very few parallels,

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.160
and yet the more we learn
about elephant society

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.560
the more parallels we see.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.280
They seem to have
very strong social bonds.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.600
Females stay in their family
groups their whole lives.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.520
And the males will stay
in their family group

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.120
until they become teenagers,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.040
and then they will move out and
they will hang out with other males

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:32.600
in what's known
as a bachelor herd.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.480
The calves get raised by their
relatives as well as their mum.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.520
So sisters, brothers, aunties,
cousins, grandma

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.960
all contribute to the raising of
the calf, much like we used to.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.200
Elephants are really amazing
at staying in touch.

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:56.360
Elephants will communicate with
each other using vocalisations,

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.240
making sounds that are
within human hearing range,

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.720
but we know that they also use
something called infrasound

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.480
which is a very low-frequency sound.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.800
And the thing about infrasound

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:08.320
is that it travels over very long
distances, it can travel many miles.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.360
And so we believe that elephants,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.160
when they separate from
each other in groups,

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.120
that they will keep in touch with
each other using infrasound

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.440
and they will use that to coordinate
their movements with each other,

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.800
eventually meeting back
up together again.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.800
For us in the Western world, and
in big cities with so many people,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.240
loneliness is a problem,
is a massive social illness.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.280
The behaviour of elephants suggests
that they are aware of death

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.880
and that they do grieve
and show emotion

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.080
when members of their family die.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.720
And they also show interest
in the remains of elephants

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.000
that they might not have known.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.360
And I quote someone
called Kyle Owens,

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.000
who was working
in Burkina Faso in 1986.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.760
He witnessed a remarkable scene

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.760
while watching a herd of eight
elephants with a tiny baby.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.280
He saw that the baby was ill,
and throughout the morning,

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:05.240
the mother tried everything
to raise the calf to its feet.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.920
He says at dusk he returned,
the baby had died.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:11.320
Its tiny body was barely visible

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.800
as the other elephants had covered
him with dirt and grass and leaves.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.160
The mother positioned herself
over her child and began to rock.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.720
Occasionally other elephants
would stroke her back affectionately

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.240
or intertwine trunks.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.280
She continued to rock
as darkness fell.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:28.800
Like elephants we grieve,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.360
it's normal and it's very common

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.040
and it's something that we're all
going to experience in life.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.600
Even though you have to go on
without this person in your life,

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:40.720
you can live through the memories
that you've had over the years.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.120
Elephants touch each other
with their trunks,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.240
and this physical contact
seems to provide comfort.

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.640
So an elephant that
has appeared distressed,

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:54.640
when another elephant
comes over to it

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.840
and seems to be comforting it using
its trunk, and touching it,

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.160
the other elephant
seems to then relax,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:01.880
and seems to show less distress.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.000
So, similar to hugging.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.960
Comfort, affection, touching,
hugging, kissing,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:11.640
is quite a reassurance that,
OK, there's some support there.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.400
You want people to feel supported,

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.720
you want them feel like they
are part of this society,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.560
they are part of this world,
and they have got a purpose here.

